<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azad Khan Afghan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azad Khan Afghan was an Afghan commander who rose to power in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>. He was one of the main rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> in the struggle for Iran. His defeat helped clear the way for the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Azad was born in Andarh, a town east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, into the Andarh clan of the Ghilzai Pashtun confederacy, the son of Sulaiman Khan. He joined the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> around 1738 and fought in his campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. When Nader was murdered in 1747, Azad was second-in-command to Amir Aslan Khan Qeroğlu Afshar, the governor of Azarbaijan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the struggle after Nader's death he went over to Nader's nephew Ebrahim Mirza, who gave him the title of khan. When Ebrahim was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrokh-shah">Shahrokh Shah</a> in 1749, Azad and his Afghan cavalry followed Mīr Sayyed Moḥammad, superintendent of the shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>, and withdrew to the western marches. Through alliances with Kurdish and Turkic chieftains and a policy of compromise with the Georgian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a>, he controlled all the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> by 1752.</p><p>Defeated at Kirkhbulakh, he could not take the lands north of the Aras, but in 1753 he annexed the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> provinces, beat a pursuing Zand army and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s home fortress of Pari near Malayer. In 1754, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> chief Fath-Ali Khan, he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a> retook it on 29 November 1754. By June 1757 he had lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> under Mohammad Hasan Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where in 1758-59 most of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a> followers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, soldiers and settlers alike, were massacred by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>. After a failed comeback he took refuge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1760, and in 1762 surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>, living out his days as an honoured pensioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>. He died in 1781 and was buried, as he had asked, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. The historian J. R. Perry describes him as brave and chivalrous but an alien in western Iran, a Sunni Afghan with no urban or tribal base and no Safavid prince to legitimise his rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azad Khan Afghan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then outmanoeuvred his partner and defeated his other rivals, including Mohammad Hasan Khan Qajar, <b>Azad Khan Afghan</b> and the Afsharid claimants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tabriz, the chief city of Azerbaijan, came under local khans after the death of Nader Shah in 1747, as rivals including <b>Azad Khan Afghan</b> and Fath Ali Khan Afshar fought over the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-tabriz">Khanate of Tabriz</a></li><li><blockquote>Fath Ali Khan Afshar, the most ambitious of the khans, fought <b>Azad Khan Afghan</b> and Karim Khan Zand for control of north-western Iran.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-urmia">Khanate of Urmia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33,66&amp;year=1771" aria-label="Afghanistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6240 965 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Azad Khan Afghan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6690 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6690 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33,66&amp;year=1771">What was here in 1771? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
